steps.body:
`unknown` also accepts any value, but you can't do anything with it until you check what it is. That makes it the right type for data you don't control. Narrow it one step at a time, and each check unlocks the next:

```typescript
const value: unknown = JSON.parse('{"port":8080}');
if (
  typeof value === "object" &&
  value !== null &&
  "port" in value &&
  typeof value.port === "number"
) {
  console.log(value.port + 1); // 8081
}
```

`typeof value === "object"` is also true for `null`, so rule that out next. `"port" in value` proves the property exists, typed `unknown`, and the last `typeof` makes it a `number`.

From here on, programs read their input from stdin. `readFileSync(0, "utf8")` from `node:fs` reads file number 0, which is stdin, and returns all of it as one string:

```stdin
hello
```

```typescript
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
const input = readFileSync(0, "utf8");
console.log(input.trim().toUpperCase()); // HELLO
```

